  1. SQL injection: This attack involves injecting malicious SQL code into a web application's input fields to extract sensitive data or cause the application to crash. The following code injects a SQL statement into a web form's "username" field:

```python
var username = document.getElementById('username').value;
var sql = 'SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = "' + username + '"';
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('GET', sql, true);
xhr.send();
```


  2. Cross-site scripting (XSS): This attack involves injecting malicious code into a web application's output to steal sensitive data or execute arbitrary code in the victim's browser. The following code injects a malicious script into a web form's "message" field:

```javascript
var message = document.getElementById('message').value;
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('POST', 'https://example.com/myservice.php', true);
xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'text/html');
xhr.send(message);
```


  3. File inclusion: This attack involves including malicious files in a web application's output to execute arbitrary code or steal sensitive data. The following code includes a malicious PHP file in a web form's "file" field:

```php
var file = document.getElementById('file').value;
var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('POST', 'https://example.com/myservice.php', true);
xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'multipart/form-data');
xhr.send(file);
```


  4. Broken authentication: This attack involves bypassing a web application's authentication mechanism to gain unauthorized access. The following code bypasses a web form's "username" and "password" fields to log in as a different user:

```javascript
